stepper.button.size {base.space.7}
stepper.border.width {control.border.width}
```
Picker:
```
picker.accent {control.accent}
picker.background {color.surface}
picker.border {color.border}
picker.radius {radius.sm}
```
Menu:
```
menu.background {color.surface}
menu.foreground {color.text.primary}
menu.border {color.border}
menu.radius {radius.md}
menu.min-width {base.size.menu-min}
menu.border.width {control.border.width}
menu.z-index {base.z.menu} internal
```
Spinner:
```
spinner.size {base.space.5}
spinner.border.width {base.border.width.3}
spinner.duration {base.duration.800} internal
```
Label:
```
label.foreground {color.text.primary}
label.font.size {font.body.size}
```
Color:
```
color.swatch.inset {control.swatch.inset}
```
Wait token path `color.swatch.inset` collides with semantic `color.*` namespace but it's component layer. Fine, path unique. CSS var `--pl-color-swatch-inset`. Plan table says "Color picker | color.swatch.inset". Good. It's a dimension; LENGTH prefix "color.swatch.inset".

Need `toggle.thumb` color while we also have `toggle.thumb.size`, etc — DTCG allows `toggle.thumb` leaf with $value AND child `.size`? That's a conflict: a token cannot also be a group with children in the tree representation. You can't have `toggle.thumb: {$value:...}` and `toggle.thumb.size: {...}` in the same nested object because `toggle.thumb` would be both a token and an object. We have this issue for:
- `toggle.thumb` (color) and `toggle.thumb.size`, `.offset`, `.travel`.
- `slider.thumb` (color) and `slider.thumb.size`, `.offset`, `.border.width`.
- `toggle.track`? `toggle.track.off` and `.on` but no leaf `toggle.track`.
- `slider.track` (color) and `slider.track.height`, `.radius` — conflict.
- `control.border` (color) and `control.border.width` — conflict! Already semantic/control has `control.border` color and `control.border.width`. Nested JSON can't have both.
- `button.border` (color) and `button.border.width` — conflict.
- `input.border` (color) and ... no input.border.width.
- `progress.track` color and no sub? progress.height separate.
- `menu.border` and `menu.border.width` — conflict.
- `stepper.border` and `stepper.border.width` — conflict.
- `input.swatch.inset` and no leaf input.swatch.
- `color.swatch.inset` no leaf color.swatch.
- `elevation.low.color` and no leaf low.
- `font.body`? no leaf font.body.
- `control.focus`? no.

In DTCG, can a token have child tokens? No, a node is either token or group. Style Dictionary may not support both. We need to represent dotted paths with separate flat keys? JSON object keys can contain dots: `"control.border": {...}` and `"control.border.width": {...}` in the same object. If we use literal dot keys, our flattener would need to treat them as paths but not split. Actually we can author files with flat keys containing dots. Our flattener takes key as a single path segment; for key `"control.border.width"`, path becomes `[...prefix, "control.border.width"]` → joining with '.' yields the same. Fine. But Style Dictionary itself may handle literal dots in keys? It might interpret them as path separators? I think SD uses object nesting, and a key with dots stays one path segment but joined with '.' in token.path, so effectively same. However reference resolution `{control.border.width}` would need to find it; SD might build nested. Could be okay. But to be safe and simplify, use nested groups and rename the conflicting color tokens? The catalog requires exact names: `control.border` and `control.border.width`, `button.border` and `button.border.width`, `menu.border` and `menu.border.width`, `stepper.border` and `stepper.border.width`, `slider.track` and `slider.track.height`, `toggle.thumb` and `toggle.thumb.size`, `slider.thumb` and `slider.thumb.size`. These conflicts are inherent in the token namespace. DTCG says token names cannot be both leaf and group? Actually JSON structure can't. Real design systems solve by flat names or by using `$value` alongside children? DTCG doesn't allow a token to be a group (a node with `$value` is a token; other keys are ignored?). So we need flat files.

Solution: author all tokens as flat keys with dot paths in a single object under a top-level group? E.g.
```json
{
  "control.border": { "$value": "{base.color.gray.300}", "$type": "color" },
  "control.border.width": { "$value": "{base.border.width.1}", "$type": "dimension" }
}
```
SD might treat each key literally and token.path = ["control.border"] or maybe split? Let me test quickly with a small SD config. If SD resolves references, `{control.border.width}` target path maybe token.path.join('.') = "control.border.width" either way. If SD splits keys with dots, it might create nested and conflict? Let's test.

Alternative: In token files, avoid conflicts by structuring them separately but use a transform? Hard.

Let's test Style Dictionary with flat dotted keys and reference resolution.

